[Music]  when a young man was found dead of  suicide in a Massachusetts parking lot  three years ago his cell phone led to a  disturbing discovery it revealed the  teens girlfriend not only suggested he  take his life but appears to have  pressured him to follow through in a  series of text messages  the young woman Michelle Carter is  charged with involuntary manslaughter  her trial resumes on Monday CBS News  legal analyst Ricky Kleman is closely  following the case and she joins us now  Ricky good morning good morning so in  this case 20 year-old Michelle Carter  has been charged with involuntary  manslaughter again in the death of  Conrad Roy what can you tell us about  the case as it stands thus far if we  look at the facts of the case it's the  question of do the facts equal the law  so what happened is you have a 17 year  old girl involved with an 18 year old  boy over a two-year period they  exchanged thousands of text messages but  as it gets closer and closer to the time  of this young man's death she encourages  him cajole him taunts him mocks him into  committing suicide this is a boy who was  depressed who was suicidal she gives him  sites to look at ideas about suicide and  the worst part of it from the  prosecution point of view is that when  she has talked him into doing this by  carbon monoxide poisoning in a car he is  on the phone with her and it goes on for  a period of time where he gets out of  the car because he's too scared and she  stalks him to get back into the car to  finish the job stays on the phone with  him doesn't call anyone hears him cry  out till his death Ricky how unusual is  it for someone to be charged with  manslaughter were not actually  physically present at the time of death  very very unusual almost unheard of this  is a case of great importance in the  Commonwealth of Massachusetts but it  will also be of great importance  throughout the country because what you  have here is someone who is considered  virtually present and that's in the same  way of her being miles and miles away  but through her pushing him through  these texts through her malicious intent  that what she is basically done has been  next to him virtually and put the gun in  his hand so they decided in  Massachusetts to charge her with  involuntary manslaughter so tell us how  that all factors into the fact that  she's not being charged with assisted  suicide but involuntary manslaughter  well in Massachusetts what you have is  one of eleven states that does not have  a law that makes it a crime to do  assisted suicide so the only thing left  for the prosecutor if they wanted to  charge her with anything was this choice  of involuntary manslaughter it's also  the reason that they've gone forward in  this case from the defense and decided  to go forward with a bench trial not a  jury trial because a jury would look at  this case the way I sound and the way  you look which is this is just terrible  this is one of the worst things I've  ever heard of but a judge might be  dispassionate enough to say what is the  law how do I follow the law  Ricky prosecutors have played videos of  Conrad Roy expressing hope in some cases  disparaging himself in others what are  they trying to do with these videos the  videos I think were very important to  the prosecution for two reasons number  one is he's not a nameless faceless  person he comes alive on the video  number two is and really important he  has hope in these videos he is a kid who  is definitely depressed has problems but  you can see the hopeful side of him he  got a job he got into school that he  wants to live he's a life for saving  Ricky and Michelle Carter's lawyers were  hoping for an acquittal on Friday they  did not get one where do we go from here  well it's in the defense case now and  the defense is going to try to say that  this is young man who would have  committed suicide anyway it's a suicide  case not a homicide case Ricky cleamen  thanks so much for being with us this  morning

